    "message": "feat(admin): give Get Started a progress bar and the Guide a rail label\n\nTwo small, defended changes rather than a rewrite. Both views were already\ndesigned -- the Guide's sticky scrollspy TOC has been there since 4.23.0 and\nthe Get Started cards since 4.29.0 -- so the useful work was auditing them\nagainst a design plan, not replacing work that holds up.\n\nGet Started: the \"N of M essential steps done\" line was plain muted text\ncarrying the only number on the page. It is now a label plus a filled track,\nwidth driven by the same ratio, with role=\"progressbar\" and the matching aria\nvalues. A bar shares the step list's own vertical axis, so it reads as the\nlist being filled in rather than a widget parked above it. A donut with a\npercentage in the middle was considered and rejected for exactly that reason,\nand because a big number in a ring is the stock answer.\n\nGuide: an \"On this page\" eyebrow above the TOC rail, reusing the existing\nglobal .eyebrow class rather than inventing type, so the rail reads as\ndeliberate wayfinding instead of a bare list of links.\n\nCut during self-critique: the progress bar briefly had an is-complete state\nthat swapped the fill to a gradient on finishing. It used only existing\ntokens but added a visual event carrying nothing the bar's fullness did not\nalready say. Removed from both the CSS and the JS branch that set it, rather\nthan left as dead weight.\n\nAlso fixed, found while auditing rather than designed: .guide-toc a\n:focus-visible changed only its background, so keyboard focus was invisible\non every TOC link while every other control in the app gets a 2px accent\noutline. And the highlight-pulse animation and the progress transition had no\nprefers-reduced-motion coverage.\n\nThe palette and type are inherited, not chosen -- these are two views inside\na shipped dashboard, and a new face or hue for two of them would break the\none thing holding the product together. That is the deliberate decision, not\nan omission.\n\nVerified: 103 tests pass; ruff, ty and node --check clean; class cross-check\nin both directions shows no orphans and confirms is-complete is gone from\nsource rather than merely unreferenced. The CSS conflict against 4.35.0's\nfailover rules was resolved by keeping both blocks; braces balance and every\nrule from each side is present.\n\nNot verified: no browser available, so the bar's proportions and the\neyebrow's spacing are read from the box model, not seen.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>",
